1
00:00:00,501 --> 00:00:03,019
Every Exploding Kittens
table has the same people.

2
00:00:03,490 --> 00:00:06,116
The one who plays every card the
second it lands in their hand.

3
00:00:06,517 --> 00:00:09,263
The one who Skips as soon as the
deck starts to feel dangerous.

4
00:00:09,683 --> 00:00:12,570
And the one who never steals
anything, because stealing feels rude.

5
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:16,129
So I turned them into bots,
sat them at the same table,

6
00:00:16,511 --> 00:00:19,500
and had them play Exploding Kittens
more than ten billion times.

7
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:22,845
The best of them won thirty
five percent of its games,

8
00:00:23,246 --> 00:00:25,390
and the worst one won
only eleven and a half.

9
00:00:26,140 --> 00:00:28,327
And the gap wasn't luck,
or some secret combo.

10
00:00:28,849 --> 00:00:32,379
It came down to five mistakes, and
most tables make every one of them.

11
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:37,022
If you've never played:
everyone draws from one deck,

12
00:00:37,423 --> 00:00:40,925
and whoever draws an Exploding Kitten
is out, unless they have a Defuse.

13
00:00:41,486 --> 00:00:44,708
Every other card helps you avoid
drawing, or makes somebody else draw.

14
00:00:45,249 --> 00:00:46,530
Last player standing wins.

15
00:00:47,041 --> 00:00:49,252
There's always one Kitten
fewer than there are players,

16
00:00:49,453 --> 00:00:51,040
so exactly one person survives.

17
00:00:51,401 --> 00:00:53,824
On your turn, you can play
as many cards as you like.

18
00:00:54,345 --> 00:00:56,227
Then you end your turn
by drawing one card.

19
00:00:56,608 --> 00:00:58,070
That draw is where people explode.

20
00:00:58,630 --> 00:01:00,172
Skip ends your turn without drawing.

21
00:01:00,552 --> 00:01:03,435
Attack does the same, and the
next player has to take two turns.

22
00:01:04,096 --> 00:01:06,058
See the Future shows
you the top three cards.

23
00:01:06,599 --> 00:01:07,660
Shuffle mixes the deck.

24
00:01:08,321 --> 00:01:10,523
Favor makes someone give
you a card of their choice.

25
00:01:11,424 --> 00:01:14,387
Two matching cat cards steal
a random card from anyone.

26
00:01:14,928 --> 00:01:16,529
And a Nope cancels almost anything.

27
00:01:16,980 --> 00:01:21,164
First, the game. My version follows the
official rulebook for the original edition.

28
00:01:21,624 --> 00:01:25,688
Fifty six cards, four Kittens,
six Defuses, Attacks that stack,

29
00:01:26,089 --> 00:01:27,470
and Nopes that cancel other Nopes.

30
00:01:28,310 --> 00:01:30,833
Before trusting it with strategy,
I checked something boring.

31
00:01:31,213 --> 00:01:34,957
With four players, the chance that the
very first card anyone draws is a Kitten is

32
00:01:35,057 --> 00:01:35,858
three in twenty three.

33
00:01:36,298 --> 00:01:38,080
That's thirteen point oh four percent.

34
00:01:38,631 --> 00:01:42,119
Across ten million shuffles, my
deck said thirteen point oh seven.

35
00:01:42,119 --> 00:01:42,919
Close enough.

36
00:01:43,330 --> 00:01:45,133
The bots only know what
a real player knows.

37
00:01:45,514 --> 00:01:47,858
Their own hand, whatever
they saw with See the Future,

38
00:01:48,198 --> 00:01:49,140
and where they hid a Kitten.

39
00:01:49,690 --> 00:01:51,332
There is only one thing
they can't do, though.

40
00:01:51,572 --> 00:01:54,816
They can't lie. Real players bluff
about where they put the Kitten.

41
00:01:55,196 --> 00:01:56,017
Mine just put it there.

42
00:01:56,358 --> 00:01:58,320
Keep that in mind, because
I'll come back to it later.

43
00:01:59,031 --> 00:01:59,967
Here's who's at the table.

44
00:02:00,481 --> 00:02:02,102
The Normal Player plays like most people.

45
00:02:02,503 --> 00:02:05,787
It dodges when it knows a Kitten is
coming, it hides Kittens at random,

46
00:02:06,207 --> 00:02:08,490
and now and then it plays a
card just because it's fun.

47
00:02:09,040 --> 00:02:11,206
The Gambler plays cards
on a whim, half the time.

48
00:02:11,587 --> 00:02:14,474
The Panicker Skips as soon as one
card in four could be a Kitten.

49
00:02:14,895 --> 00:02:16,780
The Hoarder never steals and never peeks.

50
00:02:17,311 --> 00:02:18,438
And then there's the Patient One.

51
00:02:18,478 --> 00:02:20,169
I'll get back to what it does later.

52
00:02:21,021 --> 00:02:22,962
One table, seats shuffled every game.

53
00:02:23,342 --> 00:02:25,104
The Patient One won thirty five percent.

54
00:02:25,444 --> 00:02:26,705
The Normal Player, twenty two.

55
00:02:27,205 --> 00:02:28,366
The Gambler, seventeen.

56
00:02:28,807 --> 00:02:29,927
The Panicker, fourteen.

57
00:02:30,388 --> 00:02:31,989
And the Hoarder, eleven and a half.

58
00:02:32,349 --> 00:02:33,190
A fair share is twenty.

59
00:02:34,040 --> 00:02:35,423
But a ranking doesn't tell you why.

60
00:02:35,944 --> 00:02:37,286
So I switched to a cleaner test.

61
00:02:37,587 --> 00:02:40,592
Three Normal Players, plus one test
bot that changes a single habit.

62
00:02:40,612 --> 00:02:42,716
One hundred million games for each change.

63
00:02:43,157 --> 00:02:44,860
Twenty five is the number to beat.

64
00:02:45,671 --> 00:02:47,528
Mistake number one is
playing cards for fun.

65
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:51,004
Take the Normal Player and remove
the urge to play random cards.

66
00:02:51,445 --> 00:02:54,690
Same bot otherwise. It goes from
twenty five percent to thirty percent.

67
00:02:55,080 --> 00:02:57,492
Push it the other way, with
cards on a whim half the time,

68
00:02:57,854 --> 00:02:58,899
and it falls to fourteen.

69
00:02:59,370 --> 00:03:01,595
Every Skip you spend early
is one you don't have later.

70
00:03:02,096 --> 00:03:03,318
And later is when it counts.

71
00:03:03,719 --> 00:03:05,964
At the first draw, about one
card in seven is a Kitten.

72
00:03:06,204 --> 00:03:08,750
In the last few draws, most
of what's left is Kittens.

73
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:10,702
The rulebook actually tells you this.

74
00:03:11,143 --> 00:03:13,707
Save your cards early, while
your chance of exploding is low.

75
00:03:14,408 --> 00:03:15,530
Almost nobody listens.

76
00:03:16,326 --> 00:03:17,818
Mistake number two is panicking.

77
00:03:18,690 --> 00:03:21,454
Once one card in four is a
Kitten, the deck feels dangerous,

78
00:03:21,834 --> 00:03:23,016
and most people start Skipping.

79
00:03:23,416 --> 00:03:26,360
That's the Panicker, and it only
wins sixteen percent of the time.

80
00:03:26,830 --> 00:03:28,792
Wait for a coin flip, and
you still lose ground.

81
00:03:29,173 --> 00:03:31,135
The only rule that held
up is the boring one.

82
00:03:31,496 --> 00:03:33,678
Skip or Attack when you know
the next card is a Kitten.

83
00:03:34,039 --> 00:03:34,960
Not when you feel like it.

84
00:03:35,460 --> 00:03:38,266
And if you're wondering, Attack or Skip
barely matters when you're dodging.

85
00:03:38,727 --> 00:03:40,110
The gap was a fraction of a point.

86
00:03:40,960 --> 00:03:42,362
Here's where people actually get caught.

87
00:03:42,823 --> 00:03:46,427
At a normal table of four, almost one
explosion in four happens to someone under

88
00:03:46,467 --> 00:03:48,470
Attack, stuck taking two turns in a row.

89
00:03:48,971 --> 00:03:50,734
With two players, it's almost two in five.

90
00:03:51,275 --> 00:03:54,422
But at a table of four Patient Ones,
it's only about one in thirteen.

91
00:03:54,983 --> 00:03:58,270
My guess is that patient players still
have a Skip in hand when the Attack lands.

92
00:03:58,961 --> 00:04:00,465
Which brings us to mistake number three.

93
00:04:00,806 --> 00:04:01,829
Looking at the wrong time.

94
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:05,083
The only way to know what's
coming is See the Future.

95
00:04:05,484 --> 00:04:07,767
Never using it costs you
about two and a half points.

96
00:04:08,207 --> 00:04:10,310
Using it the moment you draw
it costs about the same.

97
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:13,507
The best time is when roughly one
card in three could be a Kitten.

98
00:04:13,948 --> 00:04:16,554
Peek then. If the top
card is a Kitten, dodge.

99
00:04:16,835 --> 00:04:18,920
If it isn't, draw, and keep your Skip.

100
00:04:19,281 --> 00:04:20,303
And you can tell when that is.

101
00:04:20,704 --> 00:04:23,710
Count the cards left in the deck, and
divide by the Kittens still in it.

102
00:04:24,111 --> 00:04:26,215
About three cards per Kitten,
and it's time to look.

103
00:04:26,656 --> 00:04:28,460
Two Kittens left means six cards or fewer.

104
00:04:29,270 --> 00:04:31,419
Mistake number four happens
right after you survive.

105
00:04:32,231 --> 00:04:35,655
You drew a Kitten, you played your Defuse,
and now you can put it back anywhere you

106
00:04:35,735 --> 00:04:37,738
like. Most people tuck it in at random.

107
00:04:38,118 --> 00:04:39,140
That's exactly average.

108
00:04:39,590 --> 00:04:40,913
The bottom of the deck feels safe.

109
00:04:41,274 --> 00:04:42,497
It's actually worse than random.

110
00:04:42,978 --> 00:04:46,126
My best guess is that a Kitten at the
bottom just waits for whoever is left,

111
00:04:46,447 --> 00:04:47,529
and that could easily be you.

112
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:50,285
The best spot is the first
card the next player will draw.

113
00:04:50,826 --> 00:04:51,668
Usually that's the top.

114
00:04:52,089 --> 00:04:53,632
If you're under an
Attack, count your turns.

115
00:04:54,053 --> 00:04:55,977
One more turn to take
means second from the top.

116
00:04:56,318 --> 00:04:57,140
Two more, third.

117
00:04:57,650 --> 00:05:00,480
That one change takes a bot from
twenty five percent to thirty percent.

118
00:05:00,890 --> 00:05:02,836
If that just saved you a
Defuse, please subscribe.

119
00:05:03,057 --> 00:05:03,857
Uno is next.

120
00:05:04,550 --> 00:05:06,773
Quick confession about that
bottom of the deck result.

121
00:05:07,153 --> 00:05:09,536
The first draft of this script
had a neat explanation for it.

122
00:05:09,916 --> 00:05:12,159
Then I noticed I had never
actually tested the explanation,

123
00:05:12,319 --> 00:05:13,119
only the result.

124
00:05:13,480 --> 00:05:16,105
So I cut it. The numbers in this
video come from the simulation.

125
00:05:16,546 --> 00:05:18,590
When I give you a reason,
I'll tell you if it's a guess.

126
00:05:19,401 --> 00:05:21,279
Mistake number five is being too polite.

127
00:05:22,031 --> 00:05:24,576
Cat pairs and Favors look like
the weakest cards in the deck.

128
00:05:25,076 --> 00:05:27,641
Against a normal table, the
Patient One wins forty percent.

129
00:05:28,021 --> 00:05:30,806
Take away its pair steals,
and it drops to thirty two.

130
00:05:31,247 --> 00:05:32,930
Take away its Favors, thirty four.

131
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:35,486
That's a big part of why
the Hoarder finished last.

132
00:05:35,828 --> 00:05:38,415
Every steal is a lottery ticket
for someone else's Defuse.

133
00:05:38,796 --> 00:05:39,840
Aim it at the biggest hand.

134
00:05:40,370 --> 00:05:43,035
One warning. Any two matching
cards count as a pair,

135
00:05:43,396 --> 00:05:45,881
not just cats. Don't pair
up your Attacks or Skips.

136
00:05:46,382 --> 00:05:48,025
A bot that did that won twenty percent.

137
00:05:48,386 --> 00:05:50,330
You're trading two escapes
for one random card.

138
00:05:51,181 --> 00:05:52,649
A few smaller habits, quickly.

139
00:05:53,162 --> 00:05:55,966
When someone hits you with a
Favor, hand over your worst card,

140
00:05:56,086 --> 00:05:58,310
not a random one. That's
worth about three points.

141
00:05:58,900 --> 00:06:00,946
Nope every Attack and
every steal aimed at you.

142
00:06:01,487 --> 00:06:03,693
A bot that never Nopes
wins twenty one percent.

143
00:06:04,115 --> 00:06:05,899
One that always does wins twenty seven.

144
00:06:06,391 --> 00:06:08,980
And if you see a Kitten on
top and can't dodge, Shuffle.

145
00:06:09,321 --> 00:06:10,626
It helps, just not by much.

146
00:06:10,807 --> 00:06:11,607
Less than a point.

147
00:06:12,280 --> 00:06:13,785
Now the argument every table has.

148
00:06:14,226 --> 00:06:15,249
Does going first matter?

149
00:06:15,780 --> 00:06:16,943
With two players, it does.

150
00:06:17,364 --> 00:06:19,469
The first player wins fifty
two percent of the time.

151
00:06:19,990 --> 00:06:21,212
With five players, it flips.

152
00:06:21,673 --> 00:06:23,496
The last seat does a little
better than the first.

153
00:06:23,856 --> 00:06:26,160
You get to watch a few draws
before you risk anything.

154
00:06:27,010 --> 00:06:30,254
And if you mostly play one on one,
every one of these habits gets louder.

155
00:06:30,695 --> 00:06:34,239
Stop playing cards for fun in a two player
game, and you win sixty eight percent.

156
00:06:34,740 --> 00:06:36,161
The mistakes cost you more as well.

157
00:06:36,702 --> 00:06:39,724
Play cards on a whim half the time,
and you drop to nineteen percent.

158
00:06:40,245 --> 00:06:42,447
Skip the pair of steals, and
you're down to thirty seven.

159
00:06:43,107 --> 00:06:46,590
Even using See the Future the moment you
draw it costs you almost nine points.

160
00:06:47,381 --> 00:06:49,118
Does any of this work
against good players?

161
00:06:49,670 --> 00:06:52,293
Against a normal table, the
Patient One wins forty percent.

162
00:06:52,714 --> 00:06:54,796
Against an aggressive table
that throws Attacks around,

163
00:06:54,936 --> 00:06:57,940
fifty three. Against a table
of hoarders, half its games.

164
00:06:58,451 --> 00:07:02,375
But sit four Patient Ones at the same
table, and each one wins exactly one game in

165
00:07:02,435 --> 00:07:03,937
four. It doesn't break the game.

166
00:07:04,257 --> 00:07:06,540
It only works because other
people make the mistakes.

167
00:07:07,371 --> 00:07:08,973
It also makes games even faster.

168
00:07:09,394 --> 00:07:11,817
A normal table of four takes
around thirty two turns.

169
00:07:12,278 --> 00:07:14,040
Four Patient Ones finish in twenty five.

170
00:07:14,710 --> 00:07:16,459
So here's what ten
billion games taught me.

171
00:07:17,010 --> 00:07:18,031
Don't play cards for fun.

172
00:07:18,491 --> 00:07:19,492
Only dodge when you know.

173
00:07:19,912 --> 00:07:22,375
Peek with See the Future once
the odds reach one in three.

174
00:07:22,795 --> 00:07:24,496
Put the Kitten where
the next player draws.

175
00:07:24,717 --> 00:07:25,737
Steal from the biggest hand.

176
00:07:26,618 --> 00:07:28,600
Give away junk, and Nope
anything aimed at you.

177
00:07:29,470 --> 00:07:30,334
That's the Patient One.

178
00:07:30,655 --> 00:07:33,889
Forty percent at a table of four, and
more than half of its games with three.

179
00:07:34,411 --> 00:07:36,197
With two players, it wins sixty percent.

180
00:07:36,639 --> 00:07:39,530
With five, twenty nine and a half percent,
where a fair share is twenty percent.

181
00:07:40,214 --> 00:07:41,884
Remember the one thing my bots can't do?

182
00:07:42,367 --> 00:07:43,120
Lie.

183
00:07:43,120 --> 00:07:45,504
They never pretend the Kitten
is on top when it isn't.

184
00:07:45,804 --> 00:07:47,286
They never act nervous when they're safe.

185
00:07:47,907 --> 00:07:50,270
That part of Exploding
Kittens is still all yours.

186
00:07:51,240 --> 00:07:54,913
Next, I'm testing Uno house rules, because
one of them completely breaks the game.

187
00:07:55,275 --> 00:07:56,619
Tell me which one you think it is.
